<!--sheet-->
# Robot arm kinematics: forward, Jacobian, inverse

A planar three-joint arm is the smallest arm that shows everything a controller needs: where the tool is for given joint angles (forward kinematics), how tool speed follows joint speed (the Jacobian), where the arm loses a direction of motion (singularities), and which joint angles reach a wanted point (inverse kinematics). The formulas are derived here by hand and every one is **checked numerically in the sheet**, so you can trust them before putting them in a controller.

The arm has link lengths 0.30 m, 0.25 m and 0.20 m, so its reach is 0.75 m. Angles are in radians and every joint turns about the axis out of the page.

>> #rad

## 1. Geometry and DH parameters

Each joint follows the standard Denavit-Hartenberg convention. For a planar arm the link offset $d$ and the twist $\alpha$ are zero, so only the joint angle $\theta_i$ and the link length $a_i$ matter.

| Joint | $\theta$ | $d$ | $a$ (m) | $\alpha$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | $\theta_1$ | 0 | 0.30 | 0 |
| 2 | $\theta_2$ | 0 | 0.25 | 0 |
| 3 | $\theta_3$ | 0 | 0.20 | 0 |

L1: 0.30 m; L2: 0.25 m; L3: 0.20 m | link lengths
reach: L1 + L2 + L3 -> m | maximum reach, arm stretched out

## 2. Forward kinematics

Multiplying the three DH transforms, the tool tip is the sum of three vectors, each turned by the sum of the joint angles up to it:

$$p_x = L_1\cos\theta_1 + L_2\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2) + L_3\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2+\theta_3)$$

$$p_y = L_1\sin\theta_1 + L_2\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2) + L_3\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2+\theta_3)$$

and the tool points at angle $\phi = \theta_1+\theta_2+\theta_3$. Base configuration used in the rest of the sheet:

t1: 0.5; t2: 0.3; t3: 0.1 | joint angles, rad
fx(a, b, c): L1 * cos(a) + L2 * cos(a + b) + L3 * cos(a + b + c) -> m
fy(a, b, c): L1 * sin(a) + L2 * sin(a + b) + L3 * sin(a + b + c) -> m
p_x: fx(t1, t2, t3) -> m {key}
p_y: fy(t1, t2, t3) -> m {key}
phi_tool: t1 + t2 + t3 | tool angle, rad
dist: sqrt(p_x^2 + p_y^2) -> m | distance of the tip from the base

if dist <= reach: "PASS: the tip is within the 0.75 m reach" else: "FAIL: the tip is outside the reach"

## 3. The Jacobian

Differentiating the tip position with respect to each joint angle gives the 2 by 3 matrix that maps joint speeds to tip velocity, $\dot p = J\dot\theta$. Writing $s_1=\sin\theta_1$, $s_{12}=\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2)$, $s_{123}=\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2+\theta_3)$ and likewise $c$:

$$J = \begin{bmatrix} -(L_1 s_1 + L_2 s_{12} + L_3 s_{123}) & -(L_2 s_{12} + L_3 s_{123}) & -L_3 s_{123} \\ L_1 c_1 + L_2 c_{12} + L_3 c_{123} & L_2 c_{12} + L_3 c_{123} & L_3 c_{123} \end{bmatrix}$$

Column $j$ is the tip velocity per unit speed of joint $j$: the arm from joint $j$ to the tip, turned a quarter turn.

J11: -(L1 * sin(t1) + L2 * sin(t1 + t2) + L3 * sin(t1 + t2 + t3)) -> m
J12: -(L2 * sin(t1 + t2) + L3 * sin(t1 + t2 + t3)) -> m
J13: -L3 * sin(t1 + t2 + t3) -> m
J21: L1 * cos(t1) + L2 * cos(t1 + t2) + L3 * cos(t1 + t2 + t3) -> m
J22: L2 * cos(t1 + t2) + L3 * cos(t1 + t2 + t3) -> m
J23: L3 * cos(t1 + t2 + t3) -> m
Jm: [J11, J12, J13; J21, J22, J23] | analytic Jacobian, in m per rad

### Check against finite differences

Central differences of the forward kinematics, step $\varepsilon = 10^{-6}$ rad, give the same matrix without using any derivative formula:

eps: 1e-6
F11: (fx(t1 + eps, t2, t3) - fx(t1 - eps, t2, t3)) / (2 * eps) -> m
F12: (fx(t1, t2 + eps, t3) - fx(t1, t2 - eps, t3)) / (2 * eps) -> m
F13: (fx(t1, t2, t3 + eps) - fx(t1, t2, t3 - eps)) / (2 * eps) -> m
F21: (fy(t1 + eps, t2, t3) - fy(t1 - eps, t2, t3)) / (2 * eps) -> m
F22: (fy(t1, t2 + eps, t3) - fy(t1, t2 - eps, t3)) / (2 * eps) -> m
F23: (fy(t1, t2, t3 + eps) - fy(t1, t2, t3 - eps)) / (2 * eps) -> m
jerr: max(abs(J11 - F11), abs(J12 - F12), abs(J13 - F13), abs(J21 - F21), abs(J22 - F22), abs(J23 - F23)) -> m | largest entry difference

if jerr < 1e-8 m: "PASS: the analytic Jacobian matches finite differences to 1e-8 m" else: "FAIL: the Jacobian does not match finite differences"

## 4. Manipulability and singular configurations

The manipulability $w = \sqrt{\det(JJ^T)}$ measures how freely the tip can move in every direction; it is zero when the arm has lost a direction. By the Cauchy-Binet formula $\det(JJ^T)$ is the sum of the squared 2 by 2 minors of $J$, and for this arm the three minors are

$$m_{23} = L_2L_3\sin\theta_3,\quad m_{13} = L_1L_3\sin(\theta_2+\theta_3) + L_2L_3\sin\theta_3,\quad m_{12} = L_1L_2\sin\theta_2 + L_1L_3\sin(\theta_2+\theta_3)$$

so $w = \sqrt{m_{23}^2 + m_{13}^2 + m_{12}^2}$, and it does not depend on $\theta_1$. It vanishes only when all three minors vanish, that is $\sin\theta_2 = \sin\theta_3 = 0$: every link lies on one line, fully stretched ($\theta_2=\theta_3=0$) or folded back on itself.

a11: J11^2 + J12^2 + J13^2 -> m^2
a12: J11 * J21 + J12 * J22 + J13 * J23 -> m^2
a22: J21^2 + J22^2 + J23^2 -> m^2
w_num: sqrt(a11 * a22 - a12^2) -> m^2 | sqrt of det(J J^T), from the numbers
wm(b, c): sqrt((L2 * L3 * sin(c))^2 + (L1 * L3 * sin(b + c) + L2 * L3 * sin(c))^2 + (L1 * L2 * sin(b) + L1 * L3 * sin(b + c))^2) -> m^2
w_closed: wm(t2, t3) -> m^2 | closed form above {key}

if abs(w_num - w_closed) < 1e-12 m^2: "PASS: the closed form equals sqrt(det(J J^T))" else: "FAIL: closed form and matrix value differ"

Three configurations:

w_ext: wm(0, 0) -> m^2 | arm stretched out, a singularity
w_fold: wm(pi, 0) -> m^2 | link 2 folded back over link 1, a singularity
w_bent: wm(pi / 2, -pi / 2) -> m^2 | elbow at a right angle, wrist bent back, far from singular

if w_ext < 1e-12 m^2 and w_fold < 1e-12 m^2: "PASS: w is zero at the singular configurations" else: "FAIL: w is not zero where it should be"
if w_closed > 0.001 m^2: "PASS: the base configuration is regular" else: "FAIL: the base configuration is singular"

Manipulability against the elbow angle, with $\theta_3 = 0.4$ rad:

plot wm(x, 0.4) for x in -pi..pi

## 5. Inverse kinematics of the two-link sub-arm

For links 1 and 2 alone, a target $(x,y)$ inside the annulus $|L_1-L_2|\le r\le L_1+L_2$ gives, from the law of cosines,

$$\cos\theta_2 = \frac{x^2+y^2-L_1^2-L_2^2}{2L_1L_2}, \qquad \theta_1 = \operatorname{atan2}(y,x) - \operatorname{atan2}\big(L_2\sin\theta_2,\; L_1 + L_2\cos\theta_2\big)$$

The sign of $\theta_2 = \pm\arccos(\cdot)$ is the choice of elbow: a positive angle puts the elbow below the line from base to target (elbow down), a negative one above it (elbow up). The target is the tip of the sub-arm at $\theta_1 = 0.5$, $\theta_2 = 0.3$.

x_t: L1 * cos(0.5) + L2 * cos(0.8) -> m
y_t: L1 * sin(0.5) + L2 * sin(0.8) -> m
c2: (x_t^2 + y_t^2 - L1^2 - L2^2) / (2 * L1 * L2) | cos of the elbow angle
q2_dn: acos(c2) | elbow down
q2_up: -acos(c2) | elbow up
q1_dn: atan2(x_t / 1 m, y_t / 1 m) - atan2(L1 / 1 m + L2 / 1 m * cos(q2_dn), L2 / 1 m * sin(q2_dn))
q1_up: atan2(x_t / 1 m, y_t / 1 m) - atan2(L1 / 1 m + L2 / 1 m * cos(q2_up), L2 / 1 m * sin(q2_up))
ex_dn: L1 * cos(q1_dn) + L2 * cos(q1_dn + q2_dn) - x_t -> m
ey_dn: L1 * sin(q1_dn) + L2 * sin(q1_dn + q2_dn) - y_t -> m
ex_up: L1 * cos(q1_up) + L2 * cos(q1_up + q2_up) - x_t -> m
ey_up: L1 * sin(q1_up) + L2 * sin(q1_up + q2_up) - y_t -> m

if abs(ex_dn) + abs(ey_dn) + abs(ex_up) + abs(ey_up) < 1e-12 m: "PASS: both elbow solutions put the tip on the target" else: "FAIL: an elbow solution misses the target"
if abs(q1_dn - 0.5) < 1e-9 and abs(q2_dn - 0.3) < 1e-9: "PASS: the elbow-down solution returns the angles the target was made from" else: "FAIL: elbow-down does not return 0.5 and 0.3"

## 6. Three joints: target point and tool angle

With three joints a target point and a tool angle $\phi$ fix the problem: three equations, three unknowns. The wrist point is the target pulled back along the tool by $L_3$, and then section 5 applies to it, so a closed form exists. A controller instead iterates with damped least squares, which also behaves near singularities. The update is

$$\Delta\theta = J_a^T\left(J_aJ_a^T + \lambda^2 I\right)^{-1} e$$

where $J_a$ is the Jacobian with a third row $[1, 1, 1]$ (the tool angle) and $e$ the remaining error in $(x, y, \phi)$. The block starts from $\theta = (0.3, 0.3, 0.3)$ with damping $\lambda = 0.05$ and goes to the target $(0.45, 0.30)$ m at tool angle 1.0 rad.

```python
import math
import plotly.graph_objects as go

A1, A2, A3 = 0.30, 0.25, 0.20
TX, TY, TPHI = 0.45, 0.30, 1.0
LAM = 0.05

def fk(t):
    a, b, c = t[0], t[0] + t[1], t[0] + t[1] + t[2]
    return [A1 * math.cos(a) + A2 * math.cos(b) + A3 * math.cos(c),
            A1 * math.sin(a) + A2 * math.sin(b) + A3 * math.sin(c), c]

def jac(t):
    a, b, c = t[0], t[0] + t[1], t[0] + t[1] + t[2]
    return [[-(A1 * math.sin(a) + A2 * math.sin(b) + A3 * math.sin(c)), -(A2 * math.sin(b) + A3 * math.sin(c)), -A3 * math.sin(c)],
            [A1 * math.cos(a) + A2 * math.cos(b) + A3 * math.cos(c), A2 * math.cos(b) + A3 * math.cos(c), A3 * math.cos(c)],
            [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]]

def solve3(M, r):
    n = 3
    W = [list(M[i]) + [r[i]] for i in range(n)]
    for c in range(n):
        p = max(range(c, n), key=lambda k: abs(W[k][c]))
        W[c], W[p] = W[p], W[c]
        for k in range(c + 1, n):
            f = W[k][c] / W[c][c]
            for j in range(c, n + 1):
                W[k][j] -= f * W[c][j]
    x = [0.0] * n
    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
        x[i] = (W[i][n] - sum(W[i][j] * x[j] for j in range(i + 1, n))) / W[i][i]
    return x

th = [0.3, 0.3, 0.3]
hist = []
iters = 0
for it in range(60):
    p = fk(th)
    e = [TX - p[0], TY - p[1], TPHI - p[2]]
    err = math.sqrt(e[0] ** 2 + e[1] ** 2 + e[2] ** 2)
    hist.append(err)
    if err < 1e-10:
        break
    J = jac(th)
    JJt = [[sum(J[i][k] * J[j][k] for k in range(3)) + (LAM ** 2 if i == j else 0.0) for j in range(3)] for i in range(3)]
    y = solve3(JJt, e)
    th = [th[j] + sum(J[i][j] * y[i] for i in range(3)) for j in range(3)]
    iters = it + 1
print("iterations:", iters, " final error: %.2e" % hist[-1])
print("theta (rad):", ["%.6f" % v for v in th])
export(d1=th[0], d2=th[1], d3=th[2], d_iters=iters, d_err=hist[-1])

fig = go.Figure(go.Scatter(x=list(range(len(hist))), y=[max(h, 1e-16) for h in hist], mode="lines+markers", name="error"))
fig.update_layout(height=300, title="Damped least squares: error against iteration", xaxis_title="iteration",
                  yaxis_title="|e| (m, m, rad mixed)", yaxis_type="log", margin=dict(l=60, r=16, t=40, b=44))
fig.show()
```

The solution is checked with the sheet's own forward kinematics, and against the closed form (wrist point, then section 5 with the wrist as target):

x_T: 0.45 m; y_T: 0.30 m; phi_T: 1.0 | target point and tool angle
fkx: fx(d1, d2, d3) -> m | tip of the solution
fky: fy(d1, d2, d3) -> m
fkphi: d1 + d2 + d3
x_w: x_T - L3 * cos(phi_T) -> m | wrist point
y_w: y_T - L3 * sin(phi_T) -> m
cw: (x_w^2 + y_w^2 - L1^2 - L2^2) / (2 * L1 * L2)
b2_dn: acos(cw)
b2_up: -acos(cw)
b1_dn: atan2(x_w / 1 m, y_w / 1 m) - atan2(L1 / 1 m + L2 / 1 m * cos(b2_dn), L2 / 1 m * sin(b2_dn))
b1_up: atan2(x_w / 1 m, y_w / 1 m) - atan2(L1 / 1 m + L2 / 1 m * cos(b2_up), L2 / 1 m * sin(b2_up))
b3_dn: phi_T - b1_dn - b2_dn
b3_up: phi_T - b1_up - b2_up

if abs(fkx - x_T) < 1e-9 m and abs(fky - y_T) < 1e-9 m and abs(fkphi - phi_T) < 1e-9: "PASS: the iteration reaches the target point and the tool angle" else: "FAIL: the iteration missed the target"
if abs(d1 - b1_dn) + abs(d2 - b2_dn) + abs(d3 - b3_dn) < 1e-6 or abs(d1 - b1_up) + abs(d2 - b2_up) + abs(d3 - b3_up) < 1e-6: "PASS: the iteration equals one of the two closed-form solutions" else: "FAIL: the iteration found neither closed-form solution"

## 7. Workspace

The tip positions reached by sampling the joint angles: $\theta_1$ over a full turn and $\theta_2$, $\theta_3$ within plus or minus 2.5 rad (about 143 degrees), a typical joint limit. The colour is the manipulability, so the dark spots are where the arm is close to singular. The outer circle is the 0.75 m reach.

```python
import math
import plotly.graph_objects as go

X, Y, W = [], [], []
rmin, rmax = 9.0, 0.0
n1, n2 = 36, 21
for i in range(n1):
    a = -math.pi + 2 * math.pi * i / n1
    for j in range(n2):
        b = -2.5 + 5.0 * j / (n2 - 1)
        for k in range(n2):
            c = -2.5 + 5.0 * k / (n2 - 1)
            x = A1 * math.cos(a) + A2 * math.cos(a + b) + A3 * math.cos(a + b + c)
            y = A1 * math.sin(a) + A2 * math.sin(a + b) + A3 * math.sin(a + b + c)
            r = math.hypot(x, y)
            rmin = min(rmin, r); rmax = max(rmax, r)
            s2, s3, s23 = math.sin(b), math.sin(c), math.sin(b + c)
            m23 = A2 * A3 * s3
            m13 = A1 * A3 * s23 + A2 * A3 * s3
            m12 = A1 * A2 * s2 + A1 * A3 * s23
            X.append(round(x, 4)); Y.append(round(y, 4)); W.append(round(math.sqrt(m23**2 + m13**2 + m12**2), 5))
print("samples:", len(X), " largest tip distance %.4f m, smallest %.4f m" % (rmax, rmin))
export(ws_rmax=rmax, ws_n=len(X))
circ = [2 * math.pi * i / 120 for i in range(121)]
fig = go.Figure()
fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(x=X, y=Y, mode="markers", name="tip", marker=dict(size=3, color=W, colorscale="Viridis", showscale=True, colorbar=dict(title="w (m^2)"))))
fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(x=[0.75 * math.cos(t) for t in circ], y=[0.75 * math.sin(t) for t in circ], mode="lines", name="reach 0.75 m", line=dict(dash="dot", color="#888")))
fig.update_layout(height=540, margin=dict(l=56, r=16, t=16, b=44), legend=dict(orientation="h", y=-0.2), xaxis=dict(title="x (m)", scaleanchor="y", scaleratio=1), yaxis=dict(title="y (m)"))
fig.show()
```

if ws_rmax <= 0.75 + 1e-9: "PASS: no sampled tip is farther than the 0.75 m reach" else: "FAIL: a sampled tip lies outside the reach"

## 8. Move the joints

Drag the three sliders. The picture shows the arm over its workspace circle, and the text under it gives the tip position, the tool angle and the manipulability. Try $\theta_2 = \theta_3 = 0$: the manipulability falls to zero and the arm is stretched out.

```python
import ui

q1 = ui.slider("q1", 0.5, -3.14, 3.14, step=0.01, unit="rad", label="Joint 1")
q2 = ui.slider("q2", 0.3, -2.6, 2.6, step=0.01, unit="rad", label="Joint 2")
q3 = ui.slider("q3", 0.1, -2.6, 2.6, step=0.01, unit="rad", label="Joint 3")

@ui.panel(q1, q2, q3)
def arm(q1, q2, q3):
    a = q1; b = a + q2; c = b + q3
    pts_x = [0, A1 * math.cos(a), A1 * math.cos(a) + A2 * math.cos(b), A1 * math.cos(a) + A2 * math.cos(b) + A3 * math.cos(c)]
    pts_y = [0, A1 * math.sin(a), A1 * math.sin(a) + A2 * math.sin(b), A1 * math.sin(a) + A2 * math.sin(b) + A3 * math.sin(c)]
    t2, t3 = q2, q3
    m23 = A2 * A3 * math.sin(t3)
    m13 = A1 * A3 * math.sin(t2 + t3) + A2 * A3 * math.sin(t3)
    m12 = A1 * A2 * math.sin(t2) + A1 * A3 * math.sin(t2 + t3)
    w = math.sqrt(m23 ** 2 + m13 ** 2 + m12 ** 2)
    ring = [2 * math.pi * i / 90 for i in range(91)]
    fig = go.Figure()
    fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(x=[0.75 * math.cos(t) for t in ring], y=[0.75 * math.sin(t) for t in ring], mode="lines", line=dict(dash="dot", color="#888")))
    fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(x=pts_x, y=pts_y, mode="lines+markers", line=dict(width=5, color="#1f77b4"), marker=dict(size=[10, 12, 12, 14])))
    fig.update_layout(height=420, showlegend=False, margin=dict(l=48, r=16, t=16, b=44),
                      xaxis=dict(range=[-0.85, 0.85], title="x (m)", scaleanchor="y", scaleratio=1), yaxis=dict(range=[-0.85, 0.85], title="y (m)"))
    msg = "Tip (%.4f, %.4f) m, tool angle %.3f rad, manipulability w = %.5f m^2" % (pts_x[3], pts_y[3], q1 + q2 + q3, w)
    if w < 1e-3:
        msg += "  (near a singularity)"
    return [fig, msg]
```

## What to change

- Link lengths: edit `L1`, `L2`, `L3` in section 1 and `A1`, `A2`, `A3` in section 6. Every check follows.
- Base configuration: edit `t1`, `t2`, `t3` in section 2. Set `t2` and `t3` to 0 and the singularity check on the base configuration fails, as it should.
- Inverse kinematics: change the target `TX`, `TY`, `TPHI` in the block of section 6 and `x_T`, `y_T`, `phi_T` below it together. Start the iteration from `th = [0.3, -0.3, 0.3]` and it lands on the other elbow.
- Joint limits and sampling density of the workspace: the `2.5` and `n1`, `n2` in section 7.

Follows the cookbook example robotics_codegen.rs of the symplex library (github.com/cgorski/symplex, MIT OR Apache-2.0).
